--TEST--
`Core\Request::mount()` answers the prefix as the deployment's own plain text and the captures as the peer's `tainted` ones, so a grammar sink is open to one and shut to the other -- `rule:routing/a-request-reads-its-mount`, `rule:security/tainted-qualifier` and `rule:security/launderers-are-sink-named`, `rule:security/sink-predicate`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Request\Mount::captures, Core\Request\Mount::prefix, tools:server/what-a-program-reads-about-its-door
// A bound asserted on both sides, and the only shape that can assert it: a
// plain `string` is assignable into a `tainted` target, so a case that merely
// declared the captures `tainted` would pass over a member that had dropped
// the mark. What pins it is that one sink takes one of the pair and refuses
// the other.
var $mount = Core\Request::mount();
// The prefix is what `rule:http-server/a-request-resolves-in-five-steps` step 2 took off the path, and every mount
// row was expanded against the disk at boot -- § 2's rule that a path is never
// derived from a URL at request time -- so it is the operator's own text.
Core\Regex\Pattern $ours = Core\Regex::compile($mount->prefix());
// The captures are marked, which is § 7's own call: the peer chose which mount
// answers it, and a capture is what a multi-tenant program keys its data by.
Core\Regex\Pattern $theirs = Core\Regex::compile($mount->captures()[0]);
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
   --> case.nvs:15:50
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
